Comment and Question by MuM:
Great presentation! From your presentation, I had one question pop up in my mind, how do organizations keep their governance 
processes running smoothly when moving IAM operations to a new vendor or moving them back in-house? What governance mechanisms 
and controls do you recommend during this transition to provide accountability and reduce risk exposure?

My response to MuM:
Thank you, Muskan, for your thoughtful question. I believe transitioning IAM services to a new vendor or bringing them back 
in-house is one of the highest-risk phases of an outsourced IAM project because organizations are transferring not only 
technology but also operational responsibility, institutional knowledge, and governance processes.

From my perspective, maintaining governance during this transition requires a structured project management approach. Before 
the transition begins, organizations should conduct a comprehensive risk assessment, identify critical identities and 
privileged accounts, document ownership of IAM processes, and establish a clear governance framework that defines roles, 
responsibilities, and decision-making authority. This ensures accountability is maintained throughout the transition rather 
than being transferred informally.

During the transition, I believe project managers should implement governance controls such as a RACI matrix, formal change 
management, knowledge transfer sessions, regular governance review meetings, access validation, and a tested rollback plan. 
Where appropriate, both the outgoing and incoming providers should operate under dual oversight to verify that security 
controls remain effective and that business operations continue without disruption.

Once the transition is complete, governance should continue through independent access reviews, removal of unnecessary vendor 
privileges, compliance validation, and post-implementation audits. Organizations should also capture lessons learned to 
strengthen future outsourcing initiatives.

In my view, technology migration is only one part of a successful IAM transition. The real measure of success is the 
organization's ability to maintain effective governance, clear accountability, and continuous oversight while minimizing 
operational and security risks



 
