My response to MM:
Translating IAM Governance Requirements into Measurable Outcomes
Thank you, Mel, for your thoughtful comments and for connecting my presentation to the work of Glöckler et al. I completely agree 
with your observation. One reason I referenced their study in my literature review is that it demonstrates that IAM governance is 
multidimensional, extending beyond technology to encompass security and compliance, operational processes, organizational governance, 
and user-related requirements [1].
To answer your question, I believe organizations should translate these broad IAM requirements into measurable governance outcomes. 
In practice, every requirement should be mapped to four components:
1.	A contractual vendor obligation: what the vendor is expected to deliver.
2.	A measurable performance indicator (KPI/KRI): how success will be measured.
3.	Objective audit evidence: how compliance will be demonstrated.
4.	Continuous monitoring: how the organization will verify ongoing compliance throughout the project lifecycle.

For example, if the requirement is identity lifecycle management: 
•	The contract could require the vendor to deprovision terminated users within four hours of HR notification. 
•	The KPI would measure the percentage of accounts deprovisioned within the agreed SLA, 
•	Audit evidence would include ServiceNow workflow records, Active Directory logs, and monthly access certification reports. 
•	Continuous monitoring would automatically alert the governance team whenever an account exceeds the approved deprovisioning 
timeframe.

Similarly, if the requirement is privileged access governance, the vendor's contractual obligation may require quarterly 
privileged-access reviews and enforcement of the principle of least privilege. Compliance would be demonstrated through Privileged 
Access Management (PAM) reports, access review records, and audit logs, while continuous monitoring would identify excessive 
privileges, dormant privileged accounts, or unauthorized privilege escalation in near real time.

From a project management mindset, I see this as a requirements traceability process. During project initiation and procurement, 
governance requirements are translated into contractual obligations and security requirements. During implementation, those 
obligations become technical controls such as RBAC, MFA, PAM, and Zero Trust. During project execution and operations, they become 
measurable KPIs, Key Risk Indicators (KRIs), governance dashboards, and audit evidence, enabling project managers and executives to 
continuously monitor vendor performance rather than relying solely on periodic audits.

This approach is consistent with NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF 2.0), which encourages organizations to establish measurable 
governance outcomes and continuously monitor cybersecurity performance rather than treating compliance as a one-time exercise [2]. 
Likewise, NIST SP 800-161 Revision 1 recommends integrating supplier security requirements into contracts and maintaining continuous 
oversight of third-party performance throughout the system lifecycle [3].

I also believe that this is where many outsourced IAM projects either succeed or fail. Organizations sometimes include security 
requirements in contracts but fail to define how those requirements will be measured or evidenced. Without measurable KPIs, audit 
artifacts, and continuous monitoring, governance becomes reactive instead of proactive. By contrast, organizations that establish 
measurable obligations, require objective evidence, and continuously monitor vendor performance are better positioned to strengthen 
accountability, maintain regulatory compliance, and improve operational resilience.”

Successful outsourced IAM governance is not achieved simply by assigning responsibilities to a vendor. It requires translating governance 
principles into measurable contractual commitments, verifiable audit evidence, and continuous monitoring practices that provide ongoing 
assurance throughout the project lifecycle. This integrated governance approach was one of the central conclusions of my literature review.
Thank you again for your excellent question. I hope this provides additional insight into how organizations can operationalize IAM 
governance and transform high-level governance principles into measurable, auditable, and continuously monitored outcomes.

