My response to GC:
Integrating Governance into Project Delivery: A Shared Responsibility Model
Thank you very much, Vin, for your thoughtful comments and questions. I appreciate your observations, particularly your point about balancing 
governance with delivery pressure, as this is a challenge many organizations face when implementing outsourced IAM projects.
Regarding your first question, I believe governance should not be viewed as a separate activity that slows project delivery but rather as an 
integral part of project execution. One reason governance becomes burdensome is that organizations often introduce security reviews, compliance 
checks, and access certifications late in the project lifecycle. Instead, project managers should adopt a "security-by-design" and 
"governance-by-design" approach by embedding governance activities into each project phase rather than treating them as standalone deliverables. 
This aligns with both Agile and DevSecOps principles, in which security, compliance, and quality are incorporated into iterative development 
rather than addressed only at the end of the project. The Project Management Institute (PMI) also emphasizes tailoring governance processes 
according to project complexity and organizational risk so that governance supports delivery rather than becoming unnecessary administrative 
overhead [1].

For example, instead of conducting a large quarterly access review after deployment, an organization could automate joiner-mover-leaver workflows, 
privileged access reviews, and compliance reporting throughout the project. This reduces manual effort, enables continuous compliance, and allows 
project teams to focus on delivering business value while maintaining effective governance.
Security-wise, I do not believe the integration of governance approaches should rest solely with either the project manager, the security team, or 
the vendor. Successful outsourced IAM governance requires a shared governance model.

The project manager should coordinate governance activities, manage stakeholder communication, and ensure governance milestones are incorporated 
into the project plan. The security and IAM teams should define security requirements, Zero Trust controls, identity governance policies, and 
technical standards. The vendor is responsible for implementing the agreed contractual and technical controls while demonstrating compliance with 
established service-level agreements (SLAs). Executive management and governance committees should provide strategic oversight, approve risk 
decisions, and ensure governance objectives remain aligned with business priorities. This collaborative governance model reflects one of the 
central findings of my literature review: outsourcing transfers operational responsibilities but not organizational accountability, thereby 
making governance a shared responsibility among multiple stakeholders.

Larger organizations may also benefit from establishing a dedicated IAM Governance or Third-Party Risk Governance function. This role can oversee 
vendor performance, monitor compliance, coordinate audits, and ensure consistent governance across multiple projects, allowing project managers to 
focus on project delivery while maintaining strong security and regulatory oversight. This approach is consistent with the NIST Cybersecurity 
Framework and NIST SP 800-161 Revision 1, which emphasize clearly defined governance structures, continuous monitoring, and ongoing management of 
third-party cybersecurity risks throughout the system lifecycle [2], [3].

Most importantly, I believe successful outsourced IAM projects achieve the best outcomes when governance is integrated into day-to-day project 
management rather than treated as an additional compliance exercise. When governance is embedded into project planning, procurement, implementation, 
and operational monitoring, organizations can improve security, maintain regulatory compliance, and deliver projects more efficiently without 
compromising organizational resilience.

Thank you again for your excellent questions. They reinforce an important point from my research that effective outsourced IAM governance depends 
not only on selecting the right technical controls but also on establishing clear governance structures, shared accountability, and continuous 
collaboration among all stakeholders.

