My response to WA and RA:
Thank you, WA, for your response to Rukayat.
Governance and Accountability in Outsourced IAM: Lessons from the Change Healthcare Cyberattack
Beyond the incident response plan, the biggest challenge in maintaining accountability between a client organization and an outsourced IAM vendor 
during a security incident is the lack of clearly defined governance and ownership responsibilities. While operational IAM activities such as user 
provisioning, deprovisioning, and privileged access management may be outsourced, accountability for protecting organizational data, managing risk, 
and ensuring regulatory compliance remains with the client organization. When responsibilities are not clearly defined, both parties may assume the 
other is responsible, delaying incident response, remediation, and recovery. A good real-world example is the 2024 Change Healthcare cyberattack, 
in which compromised credentials enabled attackers to access critical systems, resulting in one of the largest healthcare cyber incidents in the 
United States. The Russian ransomware group ALPHV BlackCat encrypted and incapacitated significant portions of Change Healthcare’s functionality. 

“The cyberattack on Change Healthcare in February 2024 disrupted health care operations on an unprecedented national scale, endangering patients' 
access to care, disrupting critical clinical and eligibility operations, and threatening the solvency of the nation's provider network. It demonstrated 
that the national consequences of cyberattacks targeting mission-critical third-party providers can be even more devastating than when hospitals or 
health systems are attacked directly.” – America Hospital Association (AHA)

This is a nation-state attack, and if you pay attention to the last sentence, it seems someone is trying to talk about a third-party provider.
Although multiple technology providers and third parties were involved, the incident demonstrated that organizations cannot transfer accountability for 
protecting sensitive information simply by outsourcing operational services. It also highlighted the importance of strong identity governance, continuous 
monitoring, and executive oversight throughout the vendor relationship.

To address this challenge, I believe organizations should establish governance before an incident occurs rather than reacting afterward. This includes 
clearly defining roles and responsibilities through contracts and service-level agreements; implementing a RACI matrix for identity-related processes; 
conducting regular security and compliance audits; performing periodic access reviews; continuously monitoring privileged accounts; and maintaining a 
jointly tested incident response plan with the vendor. These governance mechanisms help ensure that both parties understand their responsibilities and 
can respond quickly and effectively to a security incident.

Successful outsourced IAM is not achieved by transferring responsibility to a vendor; it is achieved by building a strong governance 
framework that combines vendor accountability, continuous monitoring, contractual obligations, and executive oversight. As highlighted in my literature 
review, outsourcing changes who performs the operational tasks, but it does not change who is accountable for governance, compliance, and organizational 
resilience.

Thank you again for your thoughtful question. I hope my response helps clarify the governance and accountability considerations in outsourced IAM 
environments. 

