Response to WA comment:
Shared Responsibility, Organizational Accountability, and Continuous Governance in Outsourced IAM
Thank you, WA, for your thoughtful questions.
Based on both the literature and industry practice, I would argue that responsibility for a data breach in an outsourced IAM environment is often 
shared, but accountability ultimately remains with the client organization. This aligns with one of the key findings of my review: outsourcing may 
transfer operational responsibilities, but it does not transfer accountability for security, compliance, governance, or risk management outcomes. 
Organizations remain responsible for demonstrating compliance with frameworks such as ISO 27001, SOC 2, GDPR, and NIST, even when IAM operations 
are performed by a third-party provider.
The extent of responsibility depends on the root cause of the breach. For example, if a vendor fails to implement agreed security controls, neglects 
patch management, or mishandles privileged access, the vendor bears operational responsibility. However, if the organization failed to conduct adequate 
due diligence, establish governance requirements, monitor vendor performance, or enforce contractual security obligations, it also shares responsibility. 
This is why outsourced IAM is often described as a shared-responsibility model.
Regarding your second question, organizations can ensure vendors continue to follow security policies and meet security requirements by implementing 
strong governance mechanisms throughout the project lifecycle. These include:
•	Clearly defined security requirements and accountability clauses within contracts and SLAs.
•	Regular security audits and compliance assessments.
•	Continuous monitoring of access activities and privileged accounts.
•	Periodic access certification and entitlement reviews.
•	Key Risk Indicators (KRIs) and Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) for vendor performance.
•	Governance committees and scheduled security review meetings.
•	Independent assurance activities, such as SOC reports and third-party assessments.
•	Automated reporting and evidence collection to support compliance obligations.

In my view, the most effective outsourced IAM programs are those that treat vendor management as an ongoing governance activity rather than a one-time 
procurement decision. Strong oversight, continuous monitoring, and clearly defined accountability structures help ensure that security requirements 
remain effective throughout the project lifecycle while supporting organizational resilience and regulatory compliance.
Thank you again for your excellent questions.
Samuel Adeola



Comment and Question by TS:
Sam, thanks for your presentation. With reference to Outsource IAM, some challenges, from experienced has to do with access Sprawl. Where user gain 
