Response to TS comment:
Managing Access Sprawl Through Governance, Automation, and Zero Trust
Thank you for your insightful comment. I completely agree that access sprawl is a major governance and security risk in outsourced IAM environments. Access 
sprawl occurs when users accumulate permissions over time due to role changes, temporary assignments, contractor engagements, or employee exits, and those 
permissions are not promptly reviewed or revoked. This directly connects to the issue highlighted in my paper: outsourced IAM can separate operational 
control from organizational accountability, creating risks such as delayed deprovisioning, excessive privileged access, weak segregation of duties, and 
incomplete audit evidence. 

Your point about selecting vendors with automation capability is very important. However, I would add that automation alone is not enough. The vendor 
should also demonstrate strong identity governance, role-based access control, automated joiner-mover-leaver workflows, privileged access monitoring, 
access certification, audit reporting, and alignment with Zero Trust. In other words, automation should support governance rather than replace 
governance.

This is important because identity-related attacks remain a major global threat. Microsoft reported that password-based attacks accounted for more than 
99% of the over 600 million daily identity attacks, and that it blocked about 7,000 password attacks per second over the past year. CrowdStrike also 
reported that in 2024, 79% of detections were malware-free, indicating that attackers increasingly rely on valid credentials and hands-on keyboard 
activity rather than traditional malware. CrowdStrike also found that valid account abuse was the primary initial access method in 35% of cloud incidents 
in the first half of 2024. 

Business impact analysis shows that poor IAM governance can affect more than just security. It can increase audit findings, regulatory exposure, incident 
response costs, operational disruption, and reputational risk. IBM reported that the global average cost of a data breach reached USD 4.88 million in 2024, 
while organizations using IAM solutions saved up to USD 223,000 annually, and those using AI and automation saved an average of USD 1.9 million compared 
with organizations that did not. 

For these reasons, I believe organizations should evaluate outsourced IAM providers not only on their automation capabilities but also on their ability 
to support strong governance, continuous monitoring, timely deprovisioning, access reviews, privileged access oversight, and compliance reporting. These 
capabilities help ensure that access remains appropriate, risks are identified early, and security controls remain effective over time.
Ultimately, access sprawl is not just an IAM problem it is a governance, risk management, and business resilience issue. Addressing it effectively 
requires a balanced approach that combines automation, Zero Trust principles, continuous monitoring, vendor accountability, and strong management 
oversight to support both cybersecurity objectives and long-term organizational resilience.

